  "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$\u2207f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ \u2207f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
